      "title": "Threads and the Concurrency Mental Model",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 0,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Threads and the Concurrency Mental Model",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Starting a thread",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`std::thread` runs the callable ==immediately== on construction",
                "Arguments are **copied or moved** into the thread",
                "To pass a reference, wrap it in ==std::ref==",
                "A thread object is ==move-only==",
                "`hardware_concurrency()` is a core-count **hint**, may return ==0=="
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "cpp",
              "lines": [
                "void inc(int& n) { ++n; }",
                "int n = 0;",
                "std::thread t(inc, std::ref(n));",
                "t.join();  // n == 1"
              ],
              "note": "without std::ref, the thread gets a copy"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Lifetime and cancel",
              "ink": "purple"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "std::thread",
                "std::jthread (C++20)"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Joinable at destruction",
                  "{red:std::terminate}",
                  "{green:joins automatically}"
                ],
                [
                  "Fix",
                  "`join()` or `detach()`",
                  "none needed"
                ],
                [
                  "Cancel",
                  "none built in",
                  "`stop_token` + `request_stop()`"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 0
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "cpp",
              "lines": [
                "std::jthread w([](std::stop_token st) {",
                "    while (!st.stop_requested()) { /* work */ }",
                "});",
                "w.request_stop();  // dtor then joins"
              ],
              "note": "cooperative: the thread must check the token"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Exceptions",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Exception escaping the thread function calls ==std::terminate==",
                "Catch **inside** the thread, or",
                "Store `std::current_exception()` in an ==exception_ptr==, then `rethrow_exception` in the joiner"
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "Every thread must be ==joined or detached== before destruction. Prefer **jthread**."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Mutexes and Lock Wrappers",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 1,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Mutexes and Lock Wrappers",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. Lock Wrappers",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`std::mutex` has `lock()` and `unlock()`: ==never call by hand==",
                "Use an ==RAII wrapper==: unlocks on every exit, even **exceptions**"
              ],
              "style": "check",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                "lock_guard",
                "unique_lock",
                "scoped_lock"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Mutexes",
                  "1",
                  "1",
                  "**many** (C++17)"
                ],
                [
                  "Movable",
                  "no",
                  "yes",
                  "no"
                ],
                [
                  "Early unlock, `defer_lock`",
                  "no",
                  "yes",
                  "no"
                ],
                [
                  "`cv.wait`",
                  "no",
                  "==required==",
                  "no"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Many Locks, Many Readers",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "cpp",
              "lines": [
                "std::scoped_lock lk(m1, m2);  // deadlock-safe",
                "std::unique_lock u(m1, std::defer_lock);",
                "std::shared_lock rd(rw);  // many readers",
                "std::unique_lock wr(rw);  // one writer"
              ],
              "note": "rw is a std::shared_mutex"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`recursive_mutex`: same thread may ==relock==",
                "Usually hides a **design problem**"
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "Take several mutexes with ==one== `scoped_lock`, never one by one."
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Interview Point",
              "ink": "orange"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "**Why not call lock() directly?** An exception or early return skips `unlock()`.",
                "**lock_guard vs unique_lock?** unique_lock moves, defers, unlocks early.",
                "**Why scoped_lock?** It locks m1, m2 together without deadlock."
              ],
              "style": "dot"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Condition Variables",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 2,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Condition Variables",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. What a Condition Variable Does",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`std::condition_variable`: a thread ==sleeps== until another thread signals a change",
                "Waiter holds a `unique_lock` on the ==mutex== guarding the condition",
                "`wait` ==releases== the mutex while asleep, ==reacquires== it before returning"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "Lock mutex",
                    "sub": "unique_lock",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Check predicate",
                    "sub": "false? sleep",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Sleep",
                    "sub": "mutex released",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Wake + relock",
                    "sub": "recheck predicate",
                    "ink": "green"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Waiting and Notifying",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Always wait ==with a predicate==: it handles **spurious wakeups** and lost notifications",
                "Change the state ==under the mutex==, then notify"
              ],
              "style": "check",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "cpp",
              "lines": [
                "// waiter",
                "std::unique_lock lk(m);",
                "cv.wait(lk, [&]{ return ready; });",
                "// signaller",
                "{ std::lock_guard g(m); ready = true; }",
                "cv.notify_one();"
              ],
              "note": "predicate form = while(!ready) cv.wait(lk)"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Call",
                "Wakes",
                "Use when"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`notify_one()`",
                  "one waiter",
                  "one thread can proceed"
                ],
                [
                  "`notify_all()`",
                  "every waiter",
                  "several threads may proceed"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Common Mistakes",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Waiting ==without a predicate==: lost wakeup or spurious wakeup bug",
                "Changing the flag ==without the mutex==: data race, lost wakeup"
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "Remember",
              "text": "==Predicate + mutex== every time: lock, change state, notify; waiter rechecks the condition."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Atomics and Memory Order Basics",
      "role": "outline",
      "outlineIndex": 3,
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Atomics and Memory Order Basics",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "1. What std::atomic Gives You",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "`std::atomic<T>`: single operations are ==indivisible==, no data race",
                "Ops: `load` `store` `exchange` `fetch_add` `compare_exchange_strong/weak`",
                "{orange:weak} may fail ==spuriously==, so use it in a **retry loop**",
                "{green:strong} fails only when the value really differs"
              ],
              "style": "dot",
              "frame": "dashed",
              "ink": "green"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "lang": "cpp",
              "lines": [
                "std::atomic<int> x{0};",
                "int old = x.load();",
                "while (!x.compare_exchange_weak(old, old * 2)) {",
                "    // failure reloads old, then retry",
                "}"
              ],
              "note": "CAS retry loop"
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "2. Memory Order",
              "ink": "blue"
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Order",
                "Guarantee",
                "Use"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "`seq_cst` (default)",
                  "one global order of all atomic ops",
                  "**safest** choice"
                ],
                [
                  "`relaxed`",
                  "atomicity only, no ordering",
                  "plain counters"
                ],
                [
                  "`release` store",
                  "earlier writes are published",
                  "pairs with acquire"
                ],
                [
                  "`acquire` load",
                  "sees writes before the release",
                  "reader side"
                ]
              ],
              "highlightRow": 0
            },
            {
              "type": "diagram",
              "kind": "flow",
              "spec": {
                "nodes": [
                  {
                    "text": "Write data",
                    "sub": "plain write",
                    "ink": "blue"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Store release",
                    "sub": "flag = true",
                    "ink": "orange"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Load acquire",
                    "sub": "sees flag",
                    "ink": "purple"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "Read data",
                    "sub": "sees the write",
                    "ink": "green"
                  }
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "text": "3. Common Mistakes",
              "ink": "red"
            },
            {
              "type": "callout",
              "style": "sticky",
              "title": "volatile",
              "text": "`volatile` is ==not atomic== and gives **no thread safety** in C++. Use `std::atomic`."
            },
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "items": [
                "Atomic ops on a struct need it ==trivially copyable==",
                "`is_lock_free()` tells whether a lock is used"
              ],
              "style": "cross",
              "ink": "red"
            }
          ]
        }
