    {
      "title": "Check yourself",
      "role": "quiz",
      "pages": [
        {
          "title": "Check yourself",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "Does other() run while work runs?",
              "example_code": [
                "std::async(std::launch::async, work);",
                "other();"
              ],
              "a_bullets": [
                "No. The returned future is a temporary, and its destructor blocks until work finishes.",
                "Keep it: `auto f = std::async(...)`."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "What happens when run() returns?",
              "example_code": [
                "void run() {",
                "  std::thread t(work);",
                "}"
              ],
              "a_bullets": [
                "`std::terminate`, because t is still joinable at destruction.",
                "Call `t.join()` first, or use `std::jthread`."
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "qa",
              "q": "Thread A locks m1 then m2; thread B locks m2 then m1. What can go wrong, and what fixes it?",
              "a_bullets": [
                "Deadlock: each holds one mutex and waits for the other (circular wait).",
                "Lock both together with `std::scoped_lock lk(m1, m2);`."
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
